/**
 * @page vectorization_semantics Vectorization and Unsequenced Execution Semantics
 *
 * Algorithms in `raze::algorithm` accelerate eligible calls through explicit `raze::vx` SIMD,
 * compiler-assisted auto-vectorization (`autovec`), or native memory primitives (`memcpy`,
 * `memmove`, `memset`), combined with runtime CPU ISA detection (see @ref dynamic_dispatch).
 *
 * This page defines the execution-order contract, compiler auto-vectorization behavior, and
 * callable rules once for the entire algorithm library.
 *
 * @section unseq_policy Unsequenced (`unseq`) Execution Policy
 *
 * Whenever a vectorized path (explicit SIMD or compiler auto-vectorization) is selected,
 * element processing follows semantics comparable to `std::execution::unseq`:
 * - **Interleaved / lane-parallel execution:** Multiple elements may be evaluated simultaneously
 *   within a SIMD register or processed in vector-width chunks rather than strictly one by one.
 * - **No loop-carried dependencies:** Callables (`f`), predicates (`pred`), comparators (`comp`),
 *   and projections (`proj`) must not depend on cross-iteration mutable state (such as mutating
 *   a shared accumulator or writing to overlapping indices).
 * - **No cross-lane synchronization or exceptions:** Vectorized callables are expected not to
 *   acquire locks or throw exceptions across lanes.
 *
 * @section autovec_behavior Compiler Auto-Vectorization (`autovec`) & `fautovec`
 *
 * Algorithms that implement an `autovec` kernel (documented in their **Options** section) use
 * compiler-friendly `for_noalias` loops paired with runtime ISA multiversioning
 * (`AVX-512`, `AVX2`, `SSE4.2`, baseline) on **GCC, Clang, Clang-CL, ICPX, AOCC, and nvc++**:
 * - **Automatic selection:** When an algorithm's strategy selects `strategy_mode::autovec` on the
 *   active compiler (or when explicit `raze::vx` SIMD does not apply, e.g., for scalar-typed
 *   lambdas `[](int& x)`, `const` ranges, or custom projections), contiguous non-volatile ranges
 *   automatically run through the `autovec` path.
 * - **Explicit `raze::vx` SIMD priority:** On **MSVC**, on algorithms configured with
 *   `strategy_mode::manual`, or when the compile-time target ISA already satisfies the kernel's
 *   peak ISA, eligible vectorizable calls use the explicit `raze::vx` SIMD kernel instead.
 * - **Using `raze::options::fautovec`:** Designed as a zero-rewrite migration aid when moving
 *   existing generic (`auto`) lambdas with scalar-only bodies (such as `if` branches) from `std::ranges`:
 *   - Skips `raze::vx::simd` probing and instantiation completely.
 *   - Routes contiguous ranges to the compiler `autovec` kernel on GCC, Clang, ICPX, AOCC, and nvc++.
 *   - On **MSVC**, on non-contiguous ranges, or on algorithms without an `autovec` kernel,
 *     `fautovec` cleanly degrades to scalar execution (equivalent to `fscalar` without SIMD probing).
 *
 * @section scalar_fallback Strict In-Order (Scalar) Execution Guarantees
 *
 * Strict sequential element-by-element execution (disabling both explicit SIMD and compiler
 * auto-vectorization) is guaranteed whenever any of the following holds:
 * 1. **`raze::options::fscalar` is attached:** e.g., `algo[raze::options::fscalar](...)`.
 * 2. **Constant evaluation (`if consteval`):** All algorithms always execute their `constexpr`
 *    scalar path during constant evaluation.
 * 3. **Non-contiguous or `volatile` iterators:** Input/forward/bidirectional iterators, output
 *    adapters (such as `std::back_inserter`), or `volatile`-qualified buffers never enter SIMD
 *    or `autovec` loops.
 * 4. **Unsupported types or non-vectorizable callables without an `autovec` kernel:** Algorithms
 *    that only provide an explicit `raze::vx` SIMD kernel fall back to scalar whenever element
 *    types, projections (non-`std::identity`), or explicit scalar-typed callables cannot be lowered
 *    to `raze::vx::simd`.
 *
 * @section generic_callable_caveat Generic (`auto`) Callables & `simd_mask`
 *
 * @warning Because C++ concepts can only probe whether a callable's *signature* accepts
 * `raze::vx::simd<T>`—not whether its *body* compiles for SIMD types—passing an **unconstrained
 * generic lambda** (e.g., `[](auto& x)` or `[](auto x)`) causes explicit SIMD probing to succeed.
 * If the body uses scalar-only control flow such as `if (x > 0)` (where `x > 0` produces a
 * `raze::vx::simd_mask` that is **not** implicitly convertible to `bool`), compilation will fail
 * with a hard error rather than silently falling back to scalar.
 *
 * **How to handle scalar-only logic cleanly:**
 * - Use **explicit scalar parameter types**: `[](int& x) { if (x > 0) ... }` (automatically uses
 *   `autovec` on supported compilers where available, or scalar otherwise).
 * - Constrain generic parameters: `[](std::integral auto& x)` or `[](raze::vx::arithmetic_type auto& x)`.
 * - Attach `raze::options::fautovec` to skip `vx::simd` probing while retaining compiler auto-vectorization.
 * - Attach `raze::options::fscalar` to guarantee strict in-order scalar execution.
 * - For predicates, returning the comparison directly (`[](auto x) { return x > 0; }`) works in both
 *   SIMD (`simd_mask`) and scalar (`bool`) paths; an explicit `-> bool` return type forces scalar probing.
 * - To write SIMD-compatible branching inside a generic callable, use `raze::vx::select` or explicit
 *   mask reductions (`raze::vx::any_of`, `raze::vx::all_of`, `raze::vx::none_of`).
 *
 * ```cpp
 * // 1. Vectorizable generic lambda (works in explicit SIMD and autovec)
 * raze::algorithm::for_each(v, [](auto& x) { x *= 2; });
 *
 * // 2. Scalar-typed lambda: uses autovec on GCC/Clang/ICPX (scalar on MSVC)
 * raze::algorithm::for_each(v, [](int& x) { if (x > 0) x *= 2; });
 *
 * // 3. Generic lambda with scalar branching: skip vx::simd probing via fautovec
 * raze::algorithm::for_each[raze::options::fautovec](v, [](auto& x) { if (x > 0) x *= 2; });
 *
 * // 4. Guaranteed strict sequential in-order execution (stateful reduction)
 * int sum = 0;
 * raze::algorithm::for_each[raze::options::fscalar](v, [&sum](const auto& x) { sum += x; });
 *
 * // 5. HARD COMPILE ERROR: unconstrained `auto&` probed with vx::simd, `x > 0` is simd_mask
 * // raze::algorithm::for_each(v, [](auto& x) { if (x > 0) x *= 2; });
 * ```
 *
 * @see @ref dynamic_dispatch
 * @see @ref raze_options
 * @see @ref mask_semantics
 */